# LAB 4 bonus
## VDSO

We have chosen to compile the user facing part of vDSO, so we would have an ELF file that is easily relocatable. We have implemented getpid() and gettimeofday(). Because we are making use of a ELF file, vdso_sym() was implemented, which returns a function pointer for a given ELF file and function name. Thus the function walks the ELF file until it find the requested function. It looks for a ELF_PROG_DYNAMIC program header and then it searches the ELF_DYN_SYMTAB, ELF_DYN_STRTAB and ELF_DYN_HASH. After which we can strncmp the symbols. In the lib/syscall.c we search for the vdso symbol and then call it. The functions are situated in vdso/vdso.c. 

The kernel passes the vvar page pointer via the R12 register to the user task, which at the start then stores in vdso_vvar_base. This pointer is passed then in lib/syscall.c to the vdso functions which can get the required data from it.
In task_setup_vdso() the vvar page is initialised, then in task_map_vdso() the vdso ELF object file is mapped in to the process.

The entropy for the randomization of the location is 2^6.

The tests that have regex lines for free memory regions do not match because of the presense of the vDSO and vvar pages. And vma_lazy because of the loaded data page.

------------------------------------------------
# LAB 3 bonus


## Syscall
In syscall_init() all MSR are set-up, with also the per-cpu struct. Then in syscall64, swapgs is used to swap the kernelgs with the userland one, then we store the userspace stack pointer in the struct, and here r12 register so we can use it here to point it to the task struct. The rest of the procedure is filling in the task struct. sysret64 just restores the registers from the task struct, and at the end swapgs to swap gs values again, before moving to userland.

## Security features
### ASLR
The stack and code are moved to random base addresses if the executable is PIE. The "randomness" comes from rdtsc. task_relocate_elf is used to relocate the elf pointers to the correct virtual addresses. In task_load_elf, now uses a code_base which is 0 when ASLR is disabled, when enabled of course random.
For both the code and stack base, there are 2^4 spots where they can sit. The granularity is of a page size.
Because of the regexes in the other tests, that use fixed addresses, they might fail because of the ASLR and of course the addresses not matching anymore because of it.

### SMEP/SMAP
After we have init the memory, we turn SMEP and SMAP in the CR4 register. For the puts syscall we have copy the user buffer to a kernel buffer. For that copy_protected() is implemented that disables SMAP in order to do the copy and then afterwards SMAP is enabled back. Allocating and freeing seems a bit much for just a puts call, this is still safe as the call is puts and not printf, so in this stage it is already formatted; thus we use stac() and clac() in the syscall. The tests are build such so that they would not pass if SMEP/SMAP is not enabled. For SMEP the test tries to run a userland main(), without SMEP afterwards the instruction pointer goes to the user stack base, as that may have been on the kernel stack and popped. With SMEP enabled, it page faults as expected on the first instruction from userspace. For SMAP we read/write from/to the userland stack, which page faults with SMAP enabled, otherwise it does not.

### L1TF and MDS mitigations
#### MDS
For this one, we just need to do `verw %ax` when switching from kernel- to usermode, which clears the microarchitectural buffers. 
#### L1TF 
For mitigating this, we have to invert the PTE, and not just them 0'ing them out, as that could still be a valid memory address, which can still be speculatively accessed. This is has been done by declaring the PAGE_NONPRESENT macro that is used in all the cases that an entry was zero'ed out in the code. 
Other tests, check that the PTE are 0, which is why they fail when they run with this bonus turned one, as that is not the case anymore with the mitigation.


